Lobium — native clientRects farbling (seeds.clientRects).

Perturbs Element::getClientRects / GetBoundingClientRect by a stable sub-pixel delta so float
geometry fingerprints differ per profile without changing layout. Guarded on seeds.clientRects != 0.

The kernel keys the delta on the rect's own VALUES, not on a call index. That matters: honest Chrome
returns exactly equal doubles whenever geometry coincides — a child that fills its parent, one
element's bottom against the next one's top, two identically laid-out elements. Index keying gave
those independent nudges and broke every such equality, which is a handful of lines of JS to detect.
Value keying gives equal inputs equal outputs, so the relations survive; it also makes the two call
sites below agree by construction rather than by both happening to pass index 0.

The other relation honest Chrome holds exactly is getBoundingClientRect() == union(getClientRects()),
so the bounding box is computed as the union of the farbled rects instead of being farbled itself.

INTERSECTION OBSERVER TOO. entry.boundingClientRect IS getBoundingClientRect() to a page - the spec
defines it that way - but IntersectionGeometry computes it from layout rather than calling that
method, so it bypassed this hook entirely. One observer callback beside one getBoundingClientRect()
call returned two different numbers for the same box: a contradiction no honest browser produces,
and a cheaper tell than the sub-pixel noise the farbling hides. Both paths now farble with the same
kernel, the same seed and the same policy.

IntersectionGeometry calculates intersectionRatio and its threshold bucket before converting and
farbling the exposed rectangles. Leaving those cached values untouched made the ratio disagree with
intersectionRect.area / boundingClientRect.area and could deliver a callback for a threshold the
entry itself had not crossed. The hook recalculates both fields from the final exposed geometry. It
also applies the root's own policy to an explicit Element rootBounds; implicit viewport and Document
roots are not Element client rects and remain untouched.

The policy - "does this box carry HOST entropy, or is it pure CSS arithmetic?" - moved into
components/lobium_fp (lobium::ClientRectCarriesHostEntropy) precisely so the two callers cannot
drift apart again. Each caller still does its own ComputedStyle lookup and passes the two booleans.
